    {
      "id": "1556554612394299483",
      "type": "Default",
      "timestamp": "2026-10-05T06:32:18.981+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "Hey all, I will be meeting with the place that's dicing the wafers. Again this time, they're requesting we back-grind the wafers. We're looking to reduce costs as best we can. \nI have a list of questions here. Can anyone think of any better (more) questions to ask? \n\n-----\n\n- What saw street widths would allow us to dice without backgrinding?\n- What width for multi pass cut?\n- What width for single pass cut?\n- If we are able to provide 250um would that be sufficient?\n\n- What types of saws exist and what type of trade offs between them?\n\n\n- What information is most useful when planning dicing schemes\n  - Is single reticle detail, and general reticle in wafer sufficient? \n  - Would another filetype be better?   DXF, etc? \n\n- What is the primary driver of cost? Dicing time? Config set up? Saw type?\n\n- How is pricing structured: per wafer, per cut, per program setup, per die picked? \n- What's a one-time cost versus a recurring cost? \n\nThanks!!",
      "author": {
        "id": "756011607603019777",
        "name": "anfroholic",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "Andrew Wingate",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [
          {
            "id": "1423020172595298314",
